// A call in the accumulate amount, whose result changes every iteration.
//
// The closed form for an accumulate is `amount * trip count`, which is exact
// when the amount really is the same on every iteration. A call is where that
// assumption is worth nothing, and the pass has no way to check it: it can see
// the *arguments* of a call, so `n = n + scaled(i)` is rejected because `i` is
// visible, but it cannot see inside `read_string()`, and a function that returns
// a different answer each time is the ordinary case for anything that reads
// input, a clock, or a random source.
//
// The harness feeds three lines, so the loop sees 1, 2 and 3. Closed, the pass
// would call `read_string` once and multiply by three, which is 3.
//
// The pass is not the only thing that could get this wrong, so the expectation
// here is the sum the loop computes, not a claim about the shape of the code.

int main() {
    var n = 0;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < 3) {
        n = n + len(read_string());
        i = i + 1;
    }
    Console.WriteLog(n);
    return 0;
}